{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-50347",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/78f281b8039a5897ca8013324c676095b6e1f57278cbab35b05c5eb3b4360301.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & {y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, for $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ne 0,$ notice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{\u2032}|=|\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}$ become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\to -{x}^{2}\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
